00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:17.160 align:start position:0%
in this seminar on faith and  globalization we're interested in  exploring how faith and globalization  processes intersect with one another now  faith remains to be a powerful force in  the world today  indeed the number of adherents to  various world tradition religious  traditions is growing today and not just  growing but also various faith  traditions are increasingly inserting  themselves into public domain and then  the question is how does faith as  engaged for it intersect with  globalization processes what is the  nature first of the religious resurgence  what is their extent of that resurgence  and then how in particular faith is  inserting them so that itself in  globalization processes we want to look  at particular cases cases from various  spheres of life in which that becomes  evident and we're interested in  conditions under which faith can in a  salutary way inserts itself and  contribute to human flourishing so  primary questions that we are engagement  is can faith be part of globalization  processes or must it by the very nature  of the faith itself retreat in a ghetto  and if it gets engaged in the world  today in particular in globalization  processes how can that engagement be  constructive how can it be in such a way  that it shapes to the good those  powerful forces that globalization  processes are and in particular I think  significant issue is whether the faith  which by its nature defines the identity  of people and its adherents whether it  can not just define and give meaning to  the lives of people but also pull  diverse people's together so that they  can work in cooperative ways of course  the course of this sort will be covering  diverse topics and approaching it from  the vantage point of different religions  but it has limitations  and we will limit ourselves to three  religions Christianity Islam and  Buddhism and maybe making side looks at  other religions as well and also we  cannot survey the whole of the globe we  will concentrate on North America Europe  and Asia but this is only a first in the  series of courses that will be offered  at Yale of the same nature and in the  fall of 2009 and 2010 we will repeat and  this time hopefully broaden the scope  include more topics include other  religions which we have left unincluded  as well as ask different sets of  questions that emerge in the context of  the intersection between faith and  globalization processes
 

